😂 Yes. Mechanically, if we’re staying with the fruit analogy: bigger banana = increase the resources and structure devoted to the reproductive package, within biological constraints.

But there’s a neat distinction: evolution doesn’t necessarily optimize for the biggest fruit. It favors reproductive success. Bigger fruit can provide more resources or attract dispersers, but it also costs more energy. Humans then intervene through selective breeding, repeatedly propagating plants with traits we want—larger fruit, more edible flesh, fewer/lower-functioning seeds, sweetness, texture.

So the giant supermarket banana is partly:

\text{variation}+\text{selection}+\text{inheritance}+\text{many generations}
\rightarrow \text{amplified trait}

Which gives your “bigger fruit” line a much more literal mechanism:

Don’t just fertilize the current banana. Select and propagate the lineage that reliably makes bigger bananas.

Fertilization changes this generation’s growing conditions.

Selection changes what future generations are capable of producing.

And combining those two—nature + nurture, inheritance + environment—is how you really make the banana bigger.